  "prediction": "The report provides an overview of the President's budget documents, which include the annual budget, the analytical perspectives volume, and various other related publications. The budget documents are released by the Office of Management and Budget (OMB) and provide detailed information on the President's proposed spending plans for the upcoming fiscal year.\n\nThe report explains that the budget documents are released in several volumes, with the annual budget focusing on the President's revenue and spending proposals, while the analytical perspectives volume provides more detailed information on the economic and policy assumptions underlying the budget.\n\nThe report also notes that the budget documents are accompanied by various other publications, including the Green Book, which provides detailed information on the revenue provisions in the budget, and the Mid-Session Review, which updates the budget projections for the current fiscal year.\n\nThe report highlights that the budget documents are available in both print and electronic formats, and can be purchased from the Government Publishing Office (GPO) or accessed online through the GPO website or the OMB website.\n\nThe report also provides some background information on the budget process, noting that after the President's budget is released, Congress begins its own budget and appropriations process, which involves the creation of appropriations bills and the allocation of funds to various federal programs and agencies.\n\nFinally, the report provides some resources for those interested in learning more about the budget process, including the Congressional Research Service (CRS) and the legislative website Congress.gov.",
  "scored_prediction": "The report provides an overview of the President's budget documents, which include the annual budget, the analytical perspectives volume, and various other related publications. The budget documents are released by the Office of Management and Budget (OMB) and provide detailed information on the President's proposed spending plans for the upcoming fiscal year.\n\nThe report explains that the budget documents are released in several volumes, with the annual budget focusing on the President's revenue and spending proposals, while the analytical perspectives volume provides more detailed information on the economic and policy assumptions underlying the budget.\n\nThe report also notes that the budget documents are accompanied by various other publications, including the Green Book, which provides detailed information on the revenue provisions in the budget, and the Mid-Session Review, which updates the budget projections for the current fiscal year.\n\nThe report highlights that the budget documents are available in both print and electronic formats, and can be purchased from the Government Publishing Office (GPO) or accessed online through the GPO website or the OMB website.\n\nThe report also provides some background information on the budget process, noting that after the President's budget is released, Congress begins its own budget and appropriations process, which involves the creation of appropriations bills and the allocation of funds to various federal programs and agencies.\n\nFinally, the report provides some resources for those interested in learning more about the budget process, including the Congressional Research Service (CRS) and the legislative website Congress.gov.",
  "score": 31.75965615391701,
  "answers": [
    "Every year the President submits a series of volumes to Congress containing the President's proposed budget for the coming fiscal year. The President's submission is required on or after the first Monday in January, but no later than the first Monday in February (31 U.S.C. \u00a71105(a)). This year the President released the budget submission, in two installments, on March 11, and March 18, 2019. This report provides brief descriptions of the FY2020 budget volumes and related documents, together with internet addresses, Government Publishing Office (GPO) stock numbers, and prices for obtaining print copies of these publications. It also explains how to find the locations of government depository libraries, which can provide both printed copies for reference use and internet access to the online versions. This report will be updated as events warrant. Please note that neither the Congressional Research Service (CRS) nor the Library of Congress (LOC) distributes print copies of the budget documents."
  ],
